A||N||5||Heisenhund||I need a 2nd (or 3rd, etc) opinion||||5||
Z||000000||Heisenhund||09-15-2000||11:42 PM||||OK folks, lotta bad news came from my 86 GT 5 speed this week.<P>First, a quick trip upstate caused some minor overheating. When I checked the coolant level, it was sucking air. A quick check of the oil showed some milkiness and a higher than expected level. Took a couple days to get back to it. Oil looked OK, topped up the coolant & burped the engine real good. Ran fine. Gave it a bath yesterday morning, drove it later that afternoon. This morning it's harder than normal to start. Then it starts to stall on me....in heavy traffic!!! Finally make it home. Go out for an hour & a half in my son's car running errands. When I check my oil, it's WAY too high. And now there seems to be small particles of a metallic substance...<P>I think I know the answer to this one, but I want some second (or as many as I can get) opinions.<P>Heis<P>P.S.<BR>The good news is, I found out that if I break my Fiero, my wife lets me get another one!! I'm the proud owner of an 85 GT Auto now!!!  :-) ||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||09-15-2000||11:45 PM||||head gasket most likely. cracked block or head will do it to. You won't know for sure until the head is off. You might be able to figure out which side with a compresion test.||||reg||1
Z||000002||gtsonly||09-16-2000||06:01 AM||||i agree! you have a blown head gasket or a cracked head. evident by the milkyness of the oil, coolant mixing with the oil!!!  if you got metal now, your engine is on the way to the grave. compression check will confirm any thoughts!<P>------------------<BR>Chris Hatch <BR>1986 GT w/T-TOPS<BR>http://www.fiero.nl/cgi-bin/fiero/ubbmisc.cgi||||reg||1
Z||000003||-=shame=-||09-16-2000||10:21 AM||||Compression tests are nice if you have the equipment, if you don't have it like myself, just check the plugs, sometimes you'll see some kinda build-up. Of course compression testing is much better than guessing at a plug.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||09-16-2000||12:19 PM||||You can buy cheap compression guages easy enough. you want the screw in kind, not the rubber tipped job. It doesn't need to be a really good one. You should have a huge drop on the affected cylinders.<P>in this case I agree you should probly see a fouled or otherwise crapped up plug just as easy. You've definatly got someting major going on if there's obvious metal in the oil.||||reg||1
Z||000005||capt_ron||09-18-2000||12:36 PM||||Sorry to hear about your engine but the fact that you have coolant in the oil is major serious. The antifreeze will take out the bearings and trash the engine. This coolant leak is most likely from the intake and is common on the 2.8l V-6. <BR>BTW I live in Ladson.<P>------------------<BR>CAPT RON...<BR>Jack of Trades,<BR>Master of None.<BR>87 SE, 2.8L, Getrag.||||reg||1
